  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how chiral inorganic nanoparticles can be used in several areas by interacting differently with left- and right-circularly polarized light. At the top, a range of nanoparticle shapes is shown, indicating that chirality can arise from different nanoscale structures.\n\nThe lower panels highlight three main applications. In optics, a nanoparticle layer appears to distinguish between left and right CPL, producing different image outcomes, suggesting polarization-dependent transmission or display behavior. In sensing, circularly polarized light activates a nanoparticle-coated surface that binds molecules, and the graph on the right shows shifted circular dichroism signals versus wavelength, implying that molecular adsorption changes the chiral optical response and can be detected spectroscopically.\n\nIn biomedicine, chiral nanoparticles near a cell membrane are shown under circularly polarized illumination, suggesting light-triggered or chirality-dependent interactions with cells. In catalysis, molecules approach nanoparticle catalysts and then give two possible products, with one pathway marked as favored and the other disfavored, illustrating enantioselective catalysis.\n\nOverall, the image conveys that chiral inorganic nanoparticles convert structural chirality into useful optical activity, enabling polarization-sensitive optics, molecular sensing, biomedical interactions, and selective chemical transformations.",
